<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fan Zhongyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/989">989</a>–1052</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Xiwen, Zhu Yue, Wenzheng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fan Zhongyan was a statesman, writer, scholar and reformer of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Born in Wu County, he passed the imperial examination as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> in 1015 and served in regional posts and at court for over two decades, building a 150 kilometre coastal dyke and reorganising the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> against the Tangut leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a> from 1040. As vice chancellor under Emperor Renzong he led the Qingli reforms, proposed with Fu Bi in 1043 and rescinded in 1045, which later inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a>. His essay 'On Yueyang Tower' (1046) holds his often-quoted ideal that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> should be the first to worry the worries of all-under-Heaven and the last to enjoy its joys.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…died when he was three and whose mother taught him herself, passed the jinshi in 1030 at his third attempt and rose to assistant councillor; <b>Fan Zhongyan</b>, who lost his father as a baby, studied in austerity, passed in 1015 and led the Qingli reforms of the 1040s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
